--- DESCRIPTION ---
The three set tracks for Section B: samba-jazz grooves, a Blake poem for string trio, and the terms AQA's guide expects.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<figure class="sv-annotated-player sv-ap-yt" data-lesson-id="795fdb05-34e2-476e-b91b-76b6eee8a759"><div class="sv-ap-bar"><button type="button" class="sv-ap-play">&#9654;</button><span class="sv-ap-tick">0:00 / &ndash;:&ndash;&ndash;</span><div class="sv-ap-tracks"><button type="button" class="sv-ap-trackbtn sv-ap-trackbtn--on" data-track="t1" data-yt="MgeAQNBXKk0" data-dur="213">Little Fly</button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-trackbtn" data-track="t2" data-yt="0NfQmoouvTY" data-dur="300">I Know You Know</button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-trackbtn" data-track="t3" data-yt="evjfOnvIPLk" data-dur="447">I Adore You</button></div></div><div class="sv-ap-media"><div class="sv-ap-video"><div class="sv-ap-ytmount"></div></div><div class="sv-ap-wrap"><div class="sv-ap-track"><div class="sv-ap-trackfill"></div></div><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c1" data-t="0" style="left:0.00%">1<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>1 &middot; Introduction</strong>Strings enter one by one; the bass plays a chromatically descending pizzicato riff in double stopping.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c2" data-t="15.5" style="left:7.28%">2<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>2 &middot; Verse 1</strong>The vocal opens with a descending three-note pattern in E major, repeated.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c3" data-t="60.3" style="left:28.31%">3<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>3 &middot; Verse 2</strong>Same vocal melody, now with a violin countermelody and the viola recalling its own tune.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t1" data-cid="t1c4" data-t="195.8" style="left:91.92%">4<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>4 &middot; Coda</strong>Pizzicato strings, a bass improvisation, then scat singing in dialogue with it.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c1" data-t="0" style="left:0.00%">1<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>1 &middot; Groove intro</strong>A solo fretless bass riff &mdash; it avoids beat one of every bar.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c2" data-t="25" style="left:10.25%">2<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>2 &middot; Verse</strong>Very fast 4/4 &mdash; around 176 beats per minute. F major is still hidden.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c3" data-t="44.7" style="left:18.32%">3<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>3 &middot; Hook</strong>The chorus lands on F major at last &mdash; ii&ndash;V&ndash;I, every chord carrying a seventh.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c4" data-t="99" style="left:40.57%">4<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>4 &middot; Solo territory</strong>B major interlude &mdash; a vamp for piano and fretless bass improvisation.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t2" data-cid="t2c5" data-t="133.5" style="left:54.71%">5<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>5 &middot; Outro</strong>Double chorus, and out.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c1" data-t="5.4" style="left:1.21%">1<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>1 &middot; Unpitched introduction</strong>Samba instruments fade in &mdash; surdo and ocean drum. No pitch yet, so no key.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c2" data-t="41.8" style="left:9.35%">2<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>2 &middot; Pitched introduction</strong>Voice enters, then double bass playing the main groove. Vocals move in thirds.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c3" data-t="69.5" style="left:15.55%">3<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>3 &middot; Main groove</strong>Three repeating motifs layered together &mdash; this is the chorus of the piece.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c4" data-t="156.5" style="left:35.01%">4<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>4 &middot; Solo section</strong>Piano solo first, over drums and bass; the voice and double bass take over later.</span></button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-pin" data-track="t3" data-cid="t3c5" data-t="428.3" style="left:95.82%">5<span class="sv-ap-tip"><strong>5 &middot; Outro</strong>The samba instruments are left alone, then fade &mdash; no final chord to confirm a key.</span></button></div></div><figcaption class="sv-listen-credit">Official uploads, streamed from YouTube &mdash; not hosted by StudyVault. Esperanza Spalding: Little Fly (2010), I Know You Know (2008), I Adore You (2008).</figcaption></figure><div class="sv-listening"><section class="sv-card sv-card--cover" data-title="The three tracks"><h2>Your Section B choice</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q1">Your second set of study pieces is three tracks by Esperanza Spalding, an American jazz bassist, singer and composer. Two come from her album <em>Esperanza</em> (2008): <em>I Know You Know</em> and <em>I Adore You</em>. The third, <em>Little Fly</em>, comes from <em>Chamber Music Society</em> (2010).</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="The set recordings"><h2>The set recordings</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="q2" data-revision-tip="Cover the box, then write from memory all three set piece titles and the artist Esperanza Spalding."><div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact &mdash; The Set Recordings</div><p><em>I Know You Know</em> and <em>I Adore You</em> (<em>Esperanza</em>, 2008) fuse jazz with samba. <em>Little Fly</em> (<em>Chamber Music Society</em>, 2010) sets a William Blake poem for voice, double bass and string trio.</p></div><p data-narration-id="q3">All three tracks are fusions. They mix jazz harmony and improvisation, Brazilian samba rhythm and &mdash; in <em>Little Fly</em> &mdash; chamber-music strings. In 2011 Spalding became the first jazz artist to win the Grammy for Best New Artist.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Little Fly" data-track="t1" data-chapters="t1c1,t1c2,t1c3,t1c4,t1c5"><h2>Little Fly: a poem set for strings</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q4">The words are William Blake&rsquo;s poem &lsquo;The Fly&rsquo;. The scoring is <dfn class="term" data-def="Music for a small group of players, one per part.">chamber music</dfn>: voice, <dfn class="term" data-def="Plucking the strings with the fingers instead of bowing.">pizzicato</dfn> double bass, and a string trio of violin, viola and cello. That makes four string instruments &mdash; but it is not a string quartet, and naming that difference exactly earns marks.</p><p data-narration-id="q5">The structure follows Blake&rsquo;s poem and is <dfn class="term" data-def="Every verse set to the same music, with no chorus.">strophic</dfn>: an introduction<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t1">1</button>, then two verses<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="15.5" data-track="t1">2</button><button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="60.3" data-track="t1">3</button>, then a coda<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="195.8" data-track="t1">4</button>. Verses one and two of the poem make the song&rsquo;s first verse, verses three and four make the second, and the poem&rsquo;s last verse becomes the coda.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Fly: the voice" data-track="t1"><h2>Little Fly: the riff and the voice</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q6">Everything grows from the bass <dfn class="term" data-def="A short musical pattern that repeats throughout a piece.">riff</dfn> in the introduction: a chromatically descending line played pizzicato, using <dfn class="term" data-def="Playing two strings at once, so one instrument sounds two notes.">double stopping</dfn><button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t1">1</button>. In verse one the riff carries on without the double stopping, then stops altogether. The riff returns about twenty times &mdash; forty of the piece&rsquo;s sixty-three bars.</p><p data-narration-id="q7">The voice moves mostly by step and sets the words <dfn class="term" data-def="One note for each syllable of text.">syllabically</dfn>. Each verse opens with a descending three-note pattern in E major, repeated<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="15.5" data-track="t1">2</button>. At each verse-end the word &lsquo;oh&rsquo; falls in three <dfn class="term" data-def="Moving by semitones, using notes outside the key.">chromatic</dfn> steps.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-track="t1" data-title="Fly: the coda"><h2>Little Fly: verse two and the coda</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q8">Verse two adds a violin countermelody and interjections from the ensemble, and the viola recalls its own melody from the introduction<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="60.3" data-track="t1">3</button>. The trio normally plays <dfn class="term" data-def="Played with the bow.">arco</dfn>, but in the coda all three play pizzicato. A bass improvisation follows, then the voice returns scatting in dialogue with it, over a sustained string chord<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="195.8" data-track="t1">4</button>. Spalding sings in the alto range, moving into head voice with vibrato in the second half of each verse, and the strings use trills to suggest the fly.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="I Know You Know" data-track="t2" data-chapters="t2c1,t2c2,t2c3,t2c4,t2c5"><h2>I Know You Know: samba-jazz with a secret</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q9">A samba-jazz song in verse-chorus form, with improvisation sections built in<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="25" data-track="t2">2</button>. Its harmonic trick is simple to say in an exam: <strong>the home key of F major is hidden until the chorus</strong>.</p><p data-narration-id="q10">The track opens with a <dfn class="term" data-def="A single line of music, with nothing accompanying it.">monophonic</dfn> texture &mdash; a solo <dfn class="term" data-def="A bass guitar without frets, allowing smooth slides between notes.">fretless bass</dfn> riff<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t2">1</button>. The riff avoids the first beat of every one of its seventeen bars. The chords that join it dodge the chord of F, and the first sung note is G &mdash; the <dfn class="term" data-def="The second note of the scale.">supertonic</dfn>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Know: the rhythm" data-track="t2"><h2>I Know You Know: the chorus and the rhythm</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q11">The chorus finally lands on F major through the jazz progression <dfn class="term" data-def="The most common jazz cadence: chord two, then five, then home.">ii&ndash;V&ndash;I</dfn> &mdash; G minor 7, C7, F &mdash; with every chord carrying a seventh<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="44.7" data-track="t2">3</button>. The chorus melody adds <dfn class="term" data-def="Expressively lowered notes borrowed from the blues.">blue notes</dfn>, especially A flat and D flat.</p><p data-narration-id="q12">The samba lives in the rhythm section: ganza (a shaker), caixa (a Brazilian snare drum) and a triangle<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="0" data-track="t2">1</button>. Off-beat piano chords in the chorus create a double-time samba feel. <dfn class="term" data-def="Accents placed off the main beat.">Syncopations</dfn> layer into <dfn class="term" data-def="Two or more conflicting rhythmic patterns sounding together.">polyrhythm</dfn>.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-track="t2" data-title="Know: the interlude"><h2>I Know You Know: the interlude</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q13">The interlude <dfn class="term" data-def="Changes key.">modulates</dfn> to the distant key of B major, with a <dfn class="term" data-def="A short chord pattern repeated under improvisation.">vamp</dfn> for piano and bass solos<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="99" data-track="t2">4</button>. Then it snaps back to F for a double chorus<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="133.5" data-track="t2">5</button>. Production is light but nameable: some reverb, panning and EQ.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="The hidden detail" data-track="t2"><h2>I Know You Know: the hidden tonic</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="q12" data-revision-tip="Cover the box, then explain from memory why the tonic key is described as hidden in this piece."><div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact &mdash; The Hidden Tonic</div><p>The bass riff opens on dominant Cs, the chords avoid F, and the voice enters on G. F major is only confirmed by the first chord of the chorus &mdash; naming that moment is a ready-made exam answer.</p></div></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="I Adore You" data-track="t3" data-chapters="t3c1,t3c2,t3c3,t3c4,t3c5"><h2>I Adore You: scat, samba and a mode</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q14">This track has almost no conventional lyrics. The voice works in <dfn class="term" data-def="Wordless jazz singing using invented syllables.">scat</dfn> and <dfn class="term" data-def="Singing without words, on open vowel sounds.">vocalise</dfn> throughout<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="41.8" data-track="t3">2</button>. The spoken rhythm of the title phrase forms the main melodic hook.</p><p data-narration-id="q15">It opens and closes with unpitched samba percussion &mdash; surdo and ocean drum among them<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="5.4" data-track="t3">1</button>. The piece literally begins and ends in no key at all. Even when pitch arrives, the tonality stays ambiguous, with dissonant piano interjections against the voice.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Adore: structure" data-track="t3"><h2>I Adore You: structure and mode</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q16">The main groove settles into <dfn class="term" data-def="A major-sounding mode with a flattened seventh &mdash; G to G on the white notes.">G mixolydian</dfn>. The track fades out on percussion alone, so no final chord ever confirms a key &mdash; a strong exam sentence<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="428.3" data-track="t3">5</button>.</p><p data-narration-id="q17">Structure is sectional: a main groove, verses and a bridge<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="69.5" data-track="t3">3</button> that repeat and develop. The solo section is long: the piano improvises first over drums and bass, then the voice and double bass take over together<button type="button" class="sv-ap-ref" data-t="156.5" data-track="t3">4</button>. These improvisations are far more extensive than in the other two tracks.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-track="t3" data-title="Adore: the metre"><h2>I Adore You: metre and bass</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q18">The metre is the feature to name: the music alternates between 3/4 for the introductions and main groove and 2/4 for the verses and bridges, switching back and forth throughout &mdash; unusual for samba, which normally stays in 2/4 or 4/4. The low end here is double bass, where <em>I Know You Know</em> uses fretless bass guitar.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Why traditional?"><h2>Why does this count as traditional music?</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q19">Area of Study 3 includes fusions that reinterpret traditional sources in a modern style. These tracks are exactly that: samba percussion from Brazil, blues-derived notes and jazz harmony from the African-American tradition, and &mdash; in <em>Little Fly</em> &mdash; an English poem set with chamber strings.</p><p data-narration-id="q20">In Section B, frame every observation as a fusion of named traditions. That framing lifts an answer into the top band.</p></div></section><section class="sv-card" data-title="Section B"><h2>Writing about the tracks</h2><div class="sv-card-body"><p data-narration-id="q21">Marks come from three things: name the element, give its exact term, and say where in the track it happens. &lsquo;The music feels floaty&rsquo; earns nothing. &lsquo;The introduction of <em>I Know You Know</em> withholds the tonic until the chorus&rsquo; earns everything.</p><p data-narration-id="q22">Anchor each track to one signature fact: the twenty returns of <em>Little Fly</em>&rsquo;s riff with its inverted G sharp pedal; the hidden-then-revealed F major of <em>I Know You Know</em>; and the mode, scat and keyless percussion frame of <em>I Adore You</em>.</p></div></section></div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n20">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n21">Little Fly: Blake's poem in E major for voice, pizzicato double bass and string trio — a riff with an inverted G sharp pedal returning about twenty times, held dischords, and an improvised ending on a single pizzicato E.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">I Know You Know: samba-jazz at ~176 bpm that hides F major until the chorus's ii–V–I; fretless bass, ganza and caixa, blue notes, layered syncopation and a B major vamp for solos.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">I Adore You: scat throughout, unpitched percussion opening and fade-out, G mixolydian groove, dissonant jazz chords and unusual 3/4 passages — with no son clave.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">Frame everything as fusion — jazz, samba, chamber music — and answer with element, term, location.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n19">Precision with instruments earns quietly: fretless bass guitar in <em>I Know You Know</em> but double bass in <em>Little Fly</em> and <em>I Adore You</em>; ganza and caixa, not just &lsquo;shakers and drums&rsquo;; a string trio plus double bass, not a string quartet. Examiners read vague instrument lists as guesswork &mdash; the named sonority is the difference between one mark and two.</p>

